   - The disease is characterized by progressive neurological symptoms, including motor dysfunction, cognitive decline, and psychiatric disturbances.
   - The disease typically manifests in the late 20s to early 30s, but can also occur earlier or later in life.
   - The disease is fatal, with an average life expectancy of 10–15 years after onset.

