<USER_REQUEST>
What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Full body oxygen delivery is calculated by multiplying the oxygen content of the blood by the cardiac output. This process ensures that the body receives the oxygen it requires for metabolic processes. The calculation is:  
\[
\text{VO}_2 = \text{CO} \times \text{CaO}_2
\]  
Where:  
- VO₂ = volume of oxygen delivered per minute (ml/min)  
- CO = cardiac output (L/min)  
- CaO₂ = oxygen content of the blood (ml O₂/L)  

The oxygen content of the blood is calculated as:  
\[
\text{CaO}_2 = \text{Hb} \times \text{SaO}_2 \times 150 + \text{O}_2 \text{ in plasma}
\]  
Where:  
- Hb = hemoglobin concentration (g/dL)  
- SaO₂ = arterial oxygen saturation (percentage)  
- 150 = \text{150 ml O}_2 \text{ per dL of Hb at 100\% saturation}  
- O₂ in plasma = oxygen dissolved in plasma (mL O₂/L)  

Thus, the full body oxygen delivery is calculated by multiplying the cardiac output by the oxygen content of the blood, which is the product of hemoglobin concentration, arterial oxygen saturation, and the oxygen content of plasma.
