","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction:
\[
3 \text{CO}(g) + \frac{3}{2} \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 3 \text{CO}_2(g)
\]
at constant pressure and 298 K, we use the Gibbs free energy equation:
\[
\Delta S = \sum S_{\text{rxN}} - \sum S_{\text{rxP}}
\]
where \( S_{\text{rxN}} \) is the entropy of the products and \( S_{\text{rxP}} \) is the entropy of the products.

The standard entropy values (\( S_{\text{T}} \)) for the reactants and products at 298 K are:
\[
S_{\text{T}}(CO) = 151.1 \text{ J/(mol·K})} \]
\[
S_{\text{T}}(O_2) = 216.7 \text{ J/(mol·K})} \]
\[
S_{\text{T}}(CO_2) = 230.1 \text{ J/(mol·K})} \]

Substituting the given values:
\[
\Delta S = 3 \times S_{\text{T}}(CO_2)} - (3 \times S_{\text{T}}(CO) + \frac{3}{2} \times S_{\text{T}(O_2})} = \]
\[
\Delta S = 3 \times 230.1 - (3 \times 151.1 + \frac{3}{2} \times 216.7) = \]
\[
\Delta S = 690.3 - (453.3 + 320.075) = \]
\[
\Delta S = 690.3 - 773.375 = -83.075 \text{ J/K}
\]

Thus, the change in entropy is \(\boxed{-83.075 \text{ J/K}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
