   \]
   From the balanced equation, 1 mole of sulphur reacts with 1 mole of oxygen.
   The molecular weight of sulphur (S) is 32 g, and the molecular weight of oxygen (O₂) is 32 g.
   Thus, 32 g of sulphur reacts with 32 g of oxygen.
   \[
   \text{Moles of oxygen} = \frac{57.5 \, \text{g}}{32 \, \text{g/mol}} = 1.825 \, \text{mol}
   \]
   Since 1 mole of S reacts with 1 mole of O₂, the amount of sulphur required is:
   \[
   \text{Moles of sulphur} = 1.825 \, \text{mol}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Weight of sulphur} = \text{Moles of sulphur} \times \text{Molecular weight of sulphur} = 1.825 \, \text{mol} \times 32 \, \text{g/mol} = 58 \, \text{g}
   \]

