\[
\text{Fe}^{3+} + 6\text{CN}^- \rightleftharpoons \text{FeCN}_6^{4-}
\]
Given:
- K$_{eq}$ = 1.2 × 10$^{20}$
- Initial concentration of Fe$^{3+}$ = 1.0 M
- Initial concentration of CN$^{-}$ = 0 (since no KCN is added initially)
- Moles of KCN added = 0.25 moles
- Volume of the solution = 1 L (since the solution is 1.0 M and 0.25 moles of KCN is added to 1 L)
- Concentration of KCN added = 0.25 moles / 1 L = 0.25 M

Since KCN dissociates completely, the concentration of CN$^{-}$ added is 0.25 M.

Now, we can set up the equilibrium table:
| Species | Initial (mol/L) | Change (mol/L) | Final (mol/L) |
|---------|-----------------|-----------------|----------------|
| Fe$^{3+}$ | 1.0     | -x | 1.0 - x |
| CN$^{-}$ | 0.25    | -6x | 0.25 - 6x |
| FeCN$_6^{4-}$ | 0            | +x | x |

The equilibrium constant is given by:
\[
K_{eq} = \frac{[\text{FeCN}_6^{4-}]}{[\text{Fe}^{3+}] [\text{CN}^-]^6} = 1.2 \times 10^{20}
\]
Substituting the final concentrations:
\[
1.2 \times 10^{20} = \frac{x}{(1.0 - x)(0.25 - 6x)^6}
\]
