","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by the equation of the tangent line at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).  

The slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \) is the derivative of the function at \( a \), which is \( f'(a) \).  

Using the point-slope form of a line, the equation of the tangent line is:  
\[ y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a) \]  

This equation can be written as:  
\[ y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a) \]  

Thus, the equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is:  
\[ y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a) \]  

Final Answer:  
