","To prove that \( B \subseteq A \) if \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \), we can use the definition of the symmetric difference and the properties of symmetric functions.

Given that \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \), we have:
\[ (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A) \subseteq A \]

Since \( A \setminus B = A - B = A \cap B^c \) and \( B \setminus A = B - A = B \cap A^c \), we can write:
\[ (A \cap B^c) \cup (B \cap A^c) \subseteq A \]

Since \( A \cap B^c \leq A \) and \( B \cap A^c \leq B \), we can consider the two cases:
