A jogger running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track is 150 m ahead of the engine of a train running at 45 km/hr in the same direction. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. How long is the train in meters?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To determine the length of the train in meters, we first need to convert the speeds of the joger and the train to meters per second (m/s).

The jogner's speed is 9 km/hr, which is:
\[
9 \text{ km/hr} = 9 \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{3600 \text{ s}} = 9 \times \frac{10}{36} = 9 \times \frac{5}{18} = \frac{45}{18} = 2.5 \text{ m/s}.
\]
The train's speed is 45 km/hr, which is:
\[
45 \text{ km/hr} = 45 \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{3600 \text{ s}} = 45 \times \frac{10}{36} = 45 \times \frac{5}{11} = \frac{225}{36} = 12.5 \text{ m/s}.
\]
The relative speed of the train with respect to the jogner is the difference in their speeds, since they are moving in the same direction:
\[
12.5 \text{ m/s} - 2.5 \text{ m}/s = 10 \text{ m/s}.
\]
The jogner is 150 m ahead of the engine of the train. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogner. This means that the train must cover the 150 m distance between the engine and the jogner, plus the length of the train.
The distance the train travels in 25 seconds is:
\[
10 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} = 250 \text{ m}.
\]
Since the train travels 250 m in 25 seconds, this distance is the sum of the initial distance between the engine and the jogner (150 m) and the length of the train.
\[
250 \text{ m} = 150 \text{ m} + \text{Length of the train}.
\]
Thus, the length of the train is:
\[
250 \text{ m} - 150 \text{ m} = 100 \text{ m}.
\]

The length of the train is 100 meters.

Final Answer: $\boxed{100}$
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

