    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: 30
        \item Yogurt: 10
        \\
        \item Infant formula: 35
        \item Adult milk powder: 25
    \enditem{}
\end{itemize}
The proportion of each type is:
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: \(\frac{30}{100} = 0.3\)
        \item Yogurt: \(\frac{10}{100} = 0.1\)
        \item Infant formula: \(\frac{35}{100} = 0.35\)
        \item Adult milk powder: \(\frac{25}{100} = 0.25\)
    \enditem{}
\end{itemize}
The sample size is $n$. The number of samples from each type is proportional to the proportion of the type in the total number of brands.
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: \(0.3n\)
        \item Yogurt: \(0.1n\)
        \item Infant formula: \(0.35n\)
        \item Adult milk powder: \(0.25n\)
    \itemenditemize
\end{itemize}
The total sample size is:
\[
0.3n + 0.1n + 0.35n + 0.25n = 1n = n
\]
Given that 7 brands of infant formula are sampled, we have:
\[
0.35n = 7
\]
