</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To determine the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \), follow these steps:

1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}^{(0)} \)**:
   The closed-loop gain with negative feedback is given by the formula:
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
   \]
   Substituting the given values:
   \[
   A_{vf^{(0)}} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 109.09 \, \text{V/V}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new open-loop gain \( A_v' \)**:
   The gain decreases by 6 dB due to aging. The gain is reduced by a factor of \( 2^{-6} = frac{1}{2^6} = \dfrac{1}{32} \).
   \[
   A_v' = \dfrac{100}{32} = 25 \, \text{V/V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \)**:
   Using the same feedback factor \( m_v = 0.1 \), the new closed-loop gain is:
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v} = \frac{25}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{25}{0.9} \approx 26.67 \, \text{V/V}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain**:
   \[
   \Delta A_{vf} = \left( \dfrac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf^{(0)}}{ A_{vf}^{(0)}} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \dfrac{26.67 - 109.09}{109.09} \right) \times 100\%
   \]
   \[
   \Delta A_{vf} = \left( \dfrac{-82.42}{109.09} \right) \times 100\% \approx --75.2\%
   \]

Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop gain is approximately \(\boxed{-75.2\%}\).
