1. For stars with masses up to about 30 times the mass of a human (30 kg), the final outcome is a neutron star. When these stars run out of fuel, they collapse under gravity. The collapse is halted by the Pauli exclusion principle, which prevents neutrons from occupying the same quantum state. This results in a highly dense object composed primarily of neutrons, known as a neutron star.

