","The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is given by the uncertainty principle. The uncertainty principle states that the uncertainty in the energy (\(\Delta E\)) of a particle is inversely proportional to the uncertainty in its lifetime (\(\Delta t\)):

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\h}{4\pi \Delta t}
\]

where:
- \(\Delta E\) is the uncertainty in energy
- \(\h = 6.635 \times 10^{-34} \text{ eV s}\) is Planck's constant
- \(\Delta t\) is the lifetime of the particle

In this case, the lifetime \(\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}\).

First, convert \(\Delta t\) to seconds: \(\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}\).

Now, calculate \(\Delta E\):
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{6.635 \times 10^{-34} \text{ eV s}}{4\pi \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}}
\]
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{6.635 \times 10^{-34}}{8\pi \times 10^{-23}} \text{ eV}
\]
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{6.635}{8\pi} \times 10^{-11} \text{ eV}
\]
\[
\Delta E \geq 0.265 \times 1.0^{-11} \text{ eV}
\]
\[
\Delta E \geq 2.65 \times 10^{-12} \text{ eV}
\]

Now, convert \(\Delta E\) from eV to MeV:
\[
\Delta E \geq 2.65 \times 10^{-12} \text{ eV} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{9.385 \times 10^{18} \text{ eV}}
\]
\[
\Delta E \geq 2.65 \times 10^{-12} / 9.385 \times 10^{18} \text{ MeV}
\]
\[
\Delta E \geq 2.8 \times 10^{-31} \text{ MeV}
\]

