","To calculate the magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil, we use the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N I}{2R}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength (in Tesla)
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space (\( 10^{-6} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{A}cdot \text{m}cdot \text{C} \))
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil
- \( I \) is the current flowing through the coil (in Amperes)
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil (in meters)

Substituting the given values:
\[
N = 100 \, \text{turns} \\
I = 1 \, \text{A} \\
R = 0.2 \, \text{m}
\]

Substituting these values into the formula:
\[
B = \frac{(10^{-6} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{A}cdot \text{m}cdot \text{C}) \times 100 \, \text{turns} \times 1 \, \text{A}}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]
\[
B = \frac{10^{-6} \times 100 \times 1}{0.4} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B = \frac{10^{-4}}{0.4} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B = 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the circular coil is \(\boxed{2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
