   - The fine-structure coupling constant is the ratio of the electric charge to the elementary charge.  
   - It is measured with high precision by measuring the **fine-structure constant** \( \alpha \) using **atomic spectroscopy** (e.g., the fine structure of hydrogen atoms).  
   - The most precise measurements come from the **atomic spectroscopy of hydrogen atoms** (e.g., the Lamb-Dicke experiment).  
   - The current best value is \(\alpha \approx 7.94 \times 10^{-3}\).  

