","To estimate the weight of the body, we use the principle of buoyancy. The buoyant force acting on the body is equal to the weight of the air displaced by the conical body. Since the air velocity at the upper edge of the body equals the entering velocity, the air is stationary relative to the body at the upper edge. This condition ensures that the buoyant force is equal to the weight of the body, maintaining equilibrium.

1. **Flow rate and velocity at the inlet**:
   \[
   \dot{m} = 0.3 \, \text{kg/s}, \quad P = 1 \, \text{atm}, \quad T = 20^\circ\text{C}
   \]
   The density of air at 20°C and 1 atm is given by the ideal gas law:
   \[
   \rho = \frac{P}{R_0 T} = \frac{101,000 \, \text{Pa}}{820.4 \, \text{J/kg·K} \times 29.3 \, \text{K}} = 1.48 \, \text{kg/m}^3
   \]
   The inlet velocity is:
   \[
   v_1 =frac{\dot{m}}{\rho A_1}
   \]
